In our country, beyond these there are still many [creatures and things] too numerous to record, whose names and characters [cannot all be listed here].
Kugotori [puppet bird]; Umi-u [cormorant]; Tsurutori [crane]; Saikata; Suzume-taka; Tosakidori; U [cormorant]; Namerukaiiso [slippery seaweed]; and others.

Saishuu: Umimatsuto; Murasaki-nori and the above are from the Wamyosho [Japanese-Chinese name dictionary].
Choryou: Umikari, Saikaku and the above are from the Kangosho; also the Kangosho states: Kogori-na [small agar].
Okogori-na [large agar]; as in the Honcho-shiki Kangosho and the Shokukyo [Classic of Foodstuffs]; likewise below.
Shiokusa [salt grass]; Shikabigusa [deer-tail herb]; Mizugoke [water moss]; Ibukasa; Tsunomata; as in the Shokukyo below; Kurokazura; from the Honcho-meirei.

Among birds, beasts, insects, fish, grasses, and trees — among all the varieties of things — there are naturally roosters, hawks, wild ducks, and herons; among trees there are plum and others.
It is the nature of things as they naturally are: just as among fowl there are chickens, hawks, wild ducks, and herons — this is not due to age or any other [external cause].
